# A picture is laid out the same way whatever comes after it. Its cap depended on how many pictures (clips) the request
# carried: past 8 pictures or 2 clips each new one re-sized every earlier one, so its rows and key changed and the whole
# conversation stopped matching its saved state from its first picture on, for every client, on every turn of a
# screenshot loop (1080p: 2,040 rows at 8 pictures, 1,792 at 9, 312 at 50). Now the request budgets default to the count
# limits times the caps, so they never divide (setting TENSORFOLD_GLM_REQUEST_IMAGE_TOKENS or ..._VIDEO_TOKENS shares
# again, by choice); the picture cap itself stays a setting (TENSORFOLD_GLM_IMAGE_TOKENS, the model's own maximum is
# 8,000). The picture count has no cap of its own (the model has none; was 50): pictures share the request's size limit,
# now a setting (TENSORFOLD_MAX_BODY_MIB, default 96). A request too long for the window at full size is laid out once
# more with the old shared budgets (other canvases: a plain miss), and is refused only if that does not fit either.
# Each picture's and clip's identity (32 bytes) covers the vision code itself (VISION_ID: the spill tier's build id
# skips tensorfold/vision), its kind, canvas shape and pixels, and a clip's frame-pair times (two clips of the same
# frames at other times shared a key); its first 62 bits are its key, and GlmPrepared carries item_idents and
# item_seconds. The picture caches are locked (concurrent requests crashed: "OrderedDict mutated during iteration"), a
# canvas is read once, and a dropped canvas fitted again must give the pixels it was keyed by (else it leaves the cache
# and the request fails, so a retry reads it fresh). The first resize after start, which held the GIL 76-119 ms on an
# x86 core while loading kernels, runs at start (warm_fit); a picture's base64 is decoded in 1 MiB pieces (10 MiB in one
# call held the GIL 10.5 ms). mgllm, 2026-10-08.
# Tests: tests/test_9013_picture_layout.py (CPU; needs TF_GLM_TEST_MODEL_DIR and torchvision).

